    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old female is evaluated during a routine clinic visit. Her child reports that the patient requires physical assistance to select appropriate, weather-sensitive clothing, coordinate buttons and zippers, and put on her shoes, but can eat, walk, bathe, and use the toilet independently. Cognitive screening is normal. According to the Katz Index of Independence in Activities of Daily Living (ADL), how should this patient''s functional status be classified, and what is the primary clinical utility of this tool?', '["Impairment in an Instrumental ADL; the tool evaluates complex executive functions required for independent community living", "Pre-frail status; the tool is primarily a diagnostic marker for sarcopenia and underlying chronic inflammation", "Impairment in Dressing and Hygiene; the tool is used to qualify patients for skilled nursing home care under Medicare Part A", "Normal functional status; dressing is considered an instrumental ADL rather than a basic activity of daily living", "Impairment in Dressing only (Katz ADL score 5/6); the tool tracks basic self-care tasks to determine the level of daily personal care support needed and predict post-discharge needs"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Katz ADL Index evaluates six basic self-care tasks: Bathing, Dressing, Toileting, Transferring, Continence, and Feeding (mnemonic: DEATH - Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene). Requiring assistance with selecting clothes or managing fasteners represents an impairment in dressing. The Katz score is out of 6, where 6 indicates full independence and lower scores indicate increasing dependency. The primary utility of basic ADL assessment is to determine the level of assistance/nursing support needed, assess prognosis, and guide discharge planning (e.g., home care vs. assisted living vs. skilled nursing facility). Dressing is a basic ADL, not an IADL. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
