    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old man is evaluated during a routine clinic visit. His child reports that the patient requires physical assistance to select appropriate, weather-sensitive clothing, coordinate buttons and zippers, and put on his shoes, but can eat, walk, bathe, and use the toilet independently. Cognitive screening is normal. According to the Katz Index of Independence in Activities of Daily Living (ADL), how should this patient''s functional status be classified, and what is the primary clinical utility of this tool?', '["Impairment in Dressing only (Katz ADL score 5/6); the tool tracks basic self-care tasks to determine the level of daily personal care support needed and predict post-discharge needs", "Impairment in Dressing and Hygiene; the tool is used to qualify patients for skilled nursing home care under Medicare Part A", "Pre-frail status; the tool is primarily a diagnostic marker for sarcopenia and underlying chronic inflammation", "Impairment in an Instrumental ADL; the tool evaluates complex executive functions required for independent community living", "Normal functional status; dressing is considered an instrumental ADL rather than a basic activity of daily living"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Katz ADL Index evaluates six basic self-care tasks: Bathing, Dressing, Toileting, Transferring, Continence, and Feeding (mnemonic: DEATH - Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene). Requiring assistance with selecting clothes or managing fasteners represents an impairment in dressing. The Katz score is out of 6, where 6 indicates full independence and lower scores indicate increasing dependency. The primary utility of basic ADL assessment is to determine the level of assistance/nursing support needed, assess prognosis, and guide discharge planning (e.g., home care vs. assisted living vs. skilled nursing facility). Dressing is a basic ADL, not an IADL. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old woman with advanced osteoarthritis and mild cognitive decline is evaluated. The caregiver reports that while the patient can chew and swallow safely, she cannot cut her food, spread butter, or bring utensils to her mouth without dropping them, requiring full physical assistance to be fed. She is continent and independent in transferring and bathing. Which of the following correctly describes this deficit under the Katz ADL index, and what is its prognostic significance?', '["Impairment in Food Preparation; this is an instrumental ADL (IADL) that is typically lost early in the course of cognitive decline", "Impairment in Continence; the inability to feed oneself always correlates with detrusor instability and neurogenic bladder", "Impairment in Transferring; the inability to eat indicates a loss of proximal muscle strength and severe balance impairment", "Impairment in Hygiene; the patient requires assistance with feeding due to severe apraxia, which is classified under the bathing domain", "Impairment in Feeding (eating); this is typically the last basic ADL to be lost in progressive decline, and its loss is strongly associated with high 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour care"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Feeding (eating) is a basic ADL. It represents the ability to transfer food from a container/plate to one''s mouth; it does not include food preparation (which is an IADL). In the hierarchical loss of basic ADLs, feeding is typically the VERY LAST basic ADL to be lost (bathing and dressing are lost first). Consequently, the loss of feeding ability indicates advanced physical or cognitive decline and is a strong predictor of 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour nursing care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female with advanced osteoarthritis and mild cognitive decline is evaluated. The caregiver reports that while the patient can chew and swallow safely, she cannot cut her food, spread butter, or bring utensils to her mouth without dropping them, requiring full physical assistance to be fed. She is continent and independent in transferring and bathing. Which of the following correctly describes this deficit under the Katz ADL index, and what is its prognostic significance?', '["Impairment in Transferring; the inability to eat indicates a loss of proximal muscle strength and severe balance impairment", "Impairment in Feeding (eating); this is typically the last basic ADL to be lost in progressive decline, and its loss is strongly associated with high 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour care", "Impairment in Hygiene; the patient requires assistance with feeding due to severe apraxia, which is classified under the bathing domain", "Impairment in Continence; the inability to feed oneself always correlates with detrusor instability and neurogenic bladder", "Impairment in Food Preparation; this is an instrumental ADL (IADL) that is typically lost early in the course of cognitive decline"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Feeding (eating) is a basic ADL. It represents the ability to transfer food from a container/plate to one''s mouth; it does not include food preparation (which is an IADL). In the hierarchical loss of basic ADLs, feeding is typically the VERY LAST basic ADL to be lost (bathing and dressing are lost first). Consequently, the loss of feeding ability indicates advanced physical or cognitive decline and is a strong predictor of 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour nursing care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
