    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male with advanced osteoarthritis and mild cognitive decline is evaluated. The caregiver reports that while the patient can chew and swallow safely, he cannot cut his food, spread butter, or bring utensils to his mouth without dropping them, requiring full physical assistance to be fed. He is continent and independent in transferring and bathing. Which of the following correctly describes this deficit under the Katz ADL index, and what is its prognostic significance?', '["Impairment in Hygiene; the patient requires assistance with feeding due to severe apraxia, which is classified under the bathing domain", "Impairment in Continence; the inability to feed oneself always correlates with detrusor instability and neurogenic bladder", "Impairment in Feeding (eating); this is typically the last basic ADL to be lost in progressive decline, and its loss is strongly associated with high 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour care", "Impairment in Transferring; the inability to eat indicates a loss of proximal muscle strength and severe balance impairment", "Impairment in Food Preparation; this is an instrumental ADL (IADL) that is typically lost early in the course of cognitive decline"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Feeding (eating) is a basic ADL. It represents the ability to transfer food from a container/plate to one''s mouth; it does not include food preparation (which is an IADL). In the hierarchical loss of basic ADLs, feeding is typically the VERY LAST basic ADL to be lost (bathing and dressing are lost first). Consequently, the loss of feeding ability indicates advanced physical or cognitive decline and is a strong predictor of 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour nursing care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
