    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old woman with advanced osteoarthritis and mild cognitive decline is evaluated. The caregiver reports that while the patient can chew and swallow safely, she cannot cut her food, spread butter, or bring utensils to her mouth without dropping them, requiring full physical assistance to be fed. She is continent and independent in transferring and bathing. Which of the following correctly describes this deficit under the Katz ADL index, and what is its prognostic significance?', '["Impairment in Continence; the inability to feed oneself always correlates with detrusor instability and neurogenic bladder", "Impairment in Food Preparation; this is an instrumental ADL (IADL) that is typically lost early in the course of cognitive decline", "Impairment in Transferring; the inability to eat indicates a loss of proximal muscle strength and severe balance impairment", "Impairment in Hygiene; the patient requires assistance with feeding due to severe apraxia, which is classified under the bathing domain", "Impairment in Feeding (eating); this is typically the last basic ADL to be lost in progressive decline, and its loss is strongly associated with high 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour care"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Feeding (eating) is a basic ADL. It represents the ability to transfer food from a container/plate to one''s mouth; it does not include food preparation (which is an IADL). In the hierarchical loss of basic ADLs, feeding is typically the VERY LAST basic ADL to be lost (bathing and dressing are lost first). Consequently, the loss of feeding ability indicates advanced physical or cognitive decline and is a strong predictor of 1-year mortality and the need for 24-hour nursing care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female is admitted to the hospital following a mild pneumonia episode. During physical therapy evaluation, the patient is found to require weight-bearing assistance and a walker to get out of bed and transfer to a chair. Prior to admission, she was fully independent in all transfers. The team is discussing the discharge plan. Which of the following basic ADL domains is impaired, and what is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Impairment in Ambulating; recommend immediate permanent placement in a skilled nursing facility as independence is unlikely to return", "Impairment in Shopping; arrange for home-delivered meals and transportation services", "Impairment in Toileting; order a Foley catheter to prevent skin breakdown during bed rest", "Impairment in bathing; initiate occupational therapy exclusively to modify the home bathroom layout", "Impairment in Transferring; prescribe physical therapy for gait and transfer training to restore baseline function before determining long-term placement"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Transferring is a basic ADL that evaluates a patient''s ability to move in and out of bed or a chair. An acute decline in transferring due to hospitalization (deconditioning) should be managed actively with physical therapy and rehabilitation (gait and transfer training) to restore baseline function. Permanent facility placement should not be decided during an acute, reversible deconditioning episode. Toileting and bathing are distinct ADLs, and shopping is an IADL. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
