    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old man is evaluated for urinary accidents. The daughter notes that the patient is continent of urine but has frequent accidents because he cannot unbutton his trousers in time or requires assistance to clean hisself and adjust clothing after voiding. Which of the following functional domains is primarily impaired in this patient, and what is this type of incontinence called?', '["Impairment in the Hygiene ADL; this represents stress incontinence due to urethral hypermobility", "Impairment in the Toileting ADL (difficulty getting to the toilet, managing clothing, or cleaning oneself); this represents functional incontinence", "Impairment in the Accounting IADL; this represents overflow incontinence due to detrusor underactivity", "Impairment in the Dressing ADL; this represents mixed urinary incontinence due to intrinsic sphincter deficiency", "Impairment in the Continence ADL; this represents urge incontinence due to detrusor overactivity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Toileting ADL covers the ability to get to the toilet, get on and off the toilet, arrange clothing, and clean oneself. When physical or cognitive limitations (such as severe arthritis, slow gait, or difficulty managing clothing fasteners) prevent a patient from using the toilet in time despite normal physiologic bladder control, this is termed **functional incontinence**. It is managed by addressing the functional barriers (e.g., bedside commodes, clothing modifications like Velcro, scheduled toileting), not by bladder-directed medications. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 78)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female is evaluated for urinary accidents. The daughter notes that the patient is continent of urine but has frequent accidents because she cannot unbutton her trousers in time or requires assistance to clean herself and adjust clothing after voiding. Which of the following functional domains is primarily impaired in this patient, and what is this type of incontinence called?', '["Impairment in the Dressing ADL; this represents mixed urinary incontinence due to intrinsic sphincter deficiency", "Impairment in the Hygiene ADL; this represents stress incontinence due to urethral hypermobility", "Impairment in the Continence ADL; this represents urge incontinence due to detrusor overactivity", "Impairment in the Toileting ADL (difficulty getting to the toilet, managing clothing, or cleaning oneself); this represents functional incontinence", "Impairment in the Accounting IADL; this represents overflow incontinence due to detrusor underactivity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Toileting ADL covers the ability to get to the toilet, get on and off the toilet, arrange clothing, and clean oneself. When physical or cognitive limitations (such as severe arthritis, slow gait, or difficulty managing clothing fasteners) prevent a patient from using the toilet in time despite normal physiologic bladder control, this is termed **functional incontinence**. It is managed by addressing the functional barriers (e.g., bedside commodes, clothing modifications like Velcro, scheduled toileting), not by bladder-directed medications. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 78)'),
