    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old man is being assessed for safety at home. The patient is independent in eating, transferring, dressing, and using the toilet. However, due to fear of falling and severe osteoarthritis of the knees, the patient has not taken a tub bath or shower in 4 months, relying instead on partial sponge baths. He requires assistance to wash his back and lower extremities. Which of the following is the most accurate assessment of the patient''s functional status?', '["Impairment in Bathing (basic ADL), which is typically the first basic ADL to be lost in the course of functional decline; perform an occupational therapy home safety evaluation for assistive devices (e.g., tub bench, grab bars)", "Normal functional status because sponge baths are considered equivalent to full bathing in older adults", "Impairment in Hygiene (IADL); start empirical antidepressant therapy for self-neglect", "Impairment in transferring; prescribe high-dose muscle relaxants to improve knee range of motion", "Impairment in Housekeeping (IADL); recommend immediate transition to an assisted living facility"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bathing is a basic ADL and is almost always the **first basic ADL to be lost** during progressive functional decline. Requiring assistance with washing any part of the body (or avoiding bathing due to safety fears/physical limits) constitutes an impairment. The most appropriate management is to perform a home safety and occupational therapy (OT) assessment to introduce assistive devices (e.g., grab bars, shower chair, handheld shower head) to restore safe independence, rather than pushing for premature facility placement. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
