    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old man is being assessed for safety at home. The patient is independent in eating, transferring, dressing, and using the toilet. However, due to fear of falling and severe osteoarthritis of the knees, the patient has not taken a tub bath or shower in 6 months, relying instead on partial sponge baths. He requires assistance to wash his back and lower extremities. Which of the following is the most accurate assessment of the patient''s functional status?', '["Normal functional status because sponge baths are considered equivalent to full bathing in older adults", "Impairment in Housekeeping (IADL); recommend immediate transition to an assisted living facility", "Impairment in Hygiene (IADL); start empirical antidepressant therapy for self-neglect", "Impairment in Bathing (basic ADL), which is typically the first basic ADL to be lost in the course of functional decline; perform an occupational therapy home safety evaluation for assistive devices (e.g., tub bench, grab bars)", "Impairment in transferring; prescribe high-dose muscle relaxants to improve knee range of motion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bathing is a basic ADL and is almost always the **first basic ADL to be lost** during progressive functional decline. Requiring assistance with washing any part of the body (or avoiding bathing due to safety fears/physical limits) constitutes an impairment. The most appropriate management is to perform a home safety and occupational therapy (OT) assessment to introduce assistive devices (e.g., grab bars, shower chair, handheld shower head) to restore safe independence, rather than pushing for premature facility placement. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Current Geriatrics Ch. 2)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old man is brought to the clinic by his adult child, who is concerned that the patient is no longer eating well. The child notes that the patient can feed hisself and cook simple meals, but has stopped going to the grocery store because he finds planning, navigating the aisles, and carrying bags too physically exhausting and cognitively overwhelming. Which of the following best describes this functional deficit and the tool used to assess it?', '["Impairment in Transportation, which is an IADL assessed by the Mini-Cog to evaluate driving safety", "Impairment in Feeding, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Katz ADL Index; ADLs measure basic survival skills", "Impairment in Housekeeping, which is an ADL assessed by the Tinetti mobility scale", "Impairment in Shopping, which is an Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL) assessed by the Lawton IADL Scale; IADLs measure the ability to live independently in the community", "Impairment in Food Preparation, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Berg Balance Scale"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shopping is an **Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL)**. IADLs (often remembered by the mnemonic SHAFT: Shopping, Housekeeping, Accounting/Finances, Food prep, Transportation/Telephone, plus medication management) are complex activities necessary for independent community living. They are commonly assessed using the **Lawton IADL Scale**. In contrast, basic ADLs (DEATH: Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene/Bathing) represent fundamental self-care tasks. IADLs are typically lost **before** basic ADLs in progressive cognitive or physical decline. (Ref: Lawton & Brody, 1969; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old male is brought to the clinic by his adult child, who is concerned that the patient is no longer eating well. The child notes that the patient can feed hisself and cook simple meals, but has stopped going to the grocery store because he finds planning, navigating the aisles, and carrying bags too physically exhausting and cognitively overwhelming. Which of the following best describes this functional deficit and the tool used to assess it?', '["Impairment in Food Preparation, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Berg Balance Scale", "Impairment in Shopping, which is an Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL) assessed by the Lawton IADL Scale; IADLs measure the ability to live independently in the community", "Impairment in Housekeeping, which is an ADL assessed by the Tinetti mobility scale", "Impairment in Feeding, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Katz ADL Index; ADLs measure basic survival skills", "Impairment in Transportation, which is an IADL assessed by the Mini-Cog to evaluate driving safety"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Shopping is an **Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL)**. IADLs (often remembered by the mnemonic SHAFT: Shopping, Housekeeping, Accounting/Finances, Food prep, Transportation/Telephone, plus medication management) are complex activities necessary for independent community living. They are commonly assessed using the **Lawton IADL Scale**. In contrast, basic ADLs (DEATH: Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene/Bathing) represent fundamental self-care tasks. IADLs are typically lost **before** basic ADLs in progressive cognitive or physical decline. (Ref: Lawton & Brody, 1969; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
