    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male is brought to the clinic by his adult child, who is concerned that the patient is no longer eating well. The child notes that the patient can feed hisself and cook simple meals, but has stopped going to the grocery store because he finds planning, navigating the aisles, and carrying bags too physically exhausting and cognitively overwhelming. Which of the following best describes this functional deficit and the tool used to assess it?', '["Impairment in Housekeeping, which is an ADL assessed by the Tinetti mobility scale", "Impairment in Feeding, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Katz ADL Index; ADLs measure basic survival skills", "Impairment in Transportation, which is an IADL assessed by the Mini-Cog to evaluate driving safety", "Impairment in Shopping, which is an Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL) assessed by the Lawton IADL Scale; IADLs measure the ability to live independently in the community", "Impairment in Food Preparation, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Berg Balance Scale"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shopping is an **Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL)**. IADLs (often remembered by the mnemonic SHAFT: Shopping, Housekeeping, Accounting/Finances, Food prep, Transportation/Telephone, plus medication management) are complex activities necessary for independent community living. They are commonly assessed using the **Lawton IADL Scale**. In contrast, basic ADLs (DEATH: Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene/Bathing) represent fundamental self-care tasks. IADLs are typically lost **before** basic ADLs in progressive cognitive or physical decline. (Ref: Lawton & Brody, 1969; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
