    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her adult child, who is concerned that the patient is no longer eating well. The child notes that the patient can feed herself and cook simple meals, but has stopped going to the grocery store because she finds planning, navigating the aisles, and carrying bags too physically exhausting and cognitively overwhelming. Which of the following best describes this functional deficit and the tool used to assess it?', '["Impairment in Transportation, which is an IADL assessed by the Mini-Cog to evaluate driving safety", "Impairment in Feeding, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Katz ADL Index; ADLs measure basic survival skills", "Impairment in Housekeeping, which is an ADL assessed by the Tinetti mobility scale", "Impairment in Shopping, which is an Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL) assessed by the Lawton IADL Scale; IADLs measure the ability to live independently in the community", "Impairment in Food Preparation, which is a Basic Activity of Daily Living (ADL) assessed by the Berg Balance Scale"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shopping is an **Instrumental Activity of Daily Living (IADL)**. IADLs (often remembered by the mnemonic SHAFT: Shopping, Housekeeping, Accounting/Finances, Food prep, Transportation/Telephone, plus medication management) are complex activities necessary for independent community living. They are commonly assessed using the **Lawton IADL Scale**. In contrast, basic ADLs (DEATH: Dressing, Eating, Ambulating, Toileting, Hygiene/Bathing) represent fundamental self-care tasks. IADLs are typically lost **before** basic ADLs in progressive cognitive or physical decline. (Ref: Lawton & Brody, 1969; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old female lives alone in a two-story home. During a home visit, the nurse notes that the house is extremely cluttered, dust and mold are accumulating, dishes are piled in the sink, and laundry is undone. The patient admits that she can no longer vacuum, wash clothes, or clean floors due to severe dyspnea on exertion from COPD. She is independent in all basic self-care. What is the most appropriate interpretation and initial intervention?', '["The patient has an environmental safety hazard; report the patient immediately to Adult Protective Services for self-neglect", "The patient has a cognitive decline (dementia); initiate empirical donepezil therapy immediately", "The patient has a basic ADL deficit in Bathing; order an urgent home nursing referral for daily sponge baths", "The patient has an IADL deficit in Housekeeping; arrange for home health aide services or light housekeeping assistance to maintain a safe, hygienic living environment", "The patient is independent because basic ADLs are intact; no clinical interventions are warranted"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Housekeeping is an IADL. While the patient''s basic ADLs are intact, an inability to perform housekeeping due to physical limitations (such as COPD) creates a safety and health hazard (clutter, dust, mold). The appropriate intervention is to support the patient''s desire to remain at home by arranging for community resources, social services, or home care agencies to assist with light housekeeping and laundry. It does not warrant an APS report for self-neglect unless the patient is refusing essential assistance and is in imminent danger. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 14)'),
