    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman is evaluated after a home health aide discovered a scorched pot and a burner left on overnight. The patient''s spouse died recently, and the patient is now living alone. She reports eating mostly canned soup because preparing hot meals feels ''too complicated'' and she frequently forgets what she is cooking. Her basic ADLs are intact. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management step?', '["Assess for cognitive impairment, screen for geriatric depression, and recommend home-delivered meal services (e.g., Meals on Wheels) or supervised cooking to ensure nutritional safety", "Prescribe low-dose atypical antipsychotics to manage the patient''s cooking forgetfulness", "Inform the patient that she is no longer permitted to eat warm food under any circumstances", "Arrange for immediate involuntary psychiatric hospitalization due to unsafe behavior", "Recommend the immediate installation of a commercial-grade restaurant kitchen in the patient''s home"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Food preparation is an IADL. An inability to prepare meals safely (e.g., leaving the stove on, scorching cookware) poses a significant risk of house fire and malnutrition. The initial clinical steps are to evaluate the patient''s cognitive status and screen for geriatric depression (common after recent spousal bereavement) while immediately securing patient safety. This is achieved by utilizing community resources like Meals on Wheels or setting up in-home support for meal prep, and educating the family on stove safety devices (automatic shutoff devices). (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
