    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female is evaluated due to social withdrawal. The family reports that the patient has stopped attending senior center activities and misses medical appointments. The patient explains that she can no longer drive due to vision loss from macular degeneration and is unable to use her smartphone to call for rides because the text is too small and confusing. Which of the following IADL domains are impaired, and what is the best initial intervention?', '["Normal aging findings; reassure the family that social withdrawal is a natural and healthy part of late life", "Impairment in Dressing; recommend immediate occupational therapy for buttoning training", "Impairments in Transportation and Telephone use; refer the patient for low-vision rehabilitation, set up a simplified high-contrast large-button telephone, and coordinate community-based senior transit services", "Impairments in Ambulating and Eating; prescribe high-dose vitamin therapy to reverse macular degeneration", "Impairment in Transferring; recommend transition to a skilled nursing facility immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Transportation and Telephone use are key IADLs on the Lawton Scale. Visual impairment (from age-related macular degeneration) directly compromises these IADLs, leading to social isolation, depression, and missed medical care. The best intervention is a multidimensional approach: referring to low-vision rehabilitation, adapting the telephone (e.g., large-button, high-contrast, voice-activated), and arranging senior transit or paratransit services to maintain community connection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 45)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman is evaluated due to social withdrawal. The family reports that the patient has stopped attending senior center activities and misses medical appointments. The patient explains that she can no longer drive due to vision loss from macular degeneration and is unable to use her smartphone to call for rides because the text is too small and confusing. Which of the following IADL domains are impaired, and what is the best initial intervention?', '["Impairment in Transferring; recommend transition to a skilled nursing facility immediately", "Impairment in Dressing; recommend immediate occupational therapy for buttoning training", "Impairments in Ambulating and Eating; prescribe high-dose vitamin therapy to reverse macular degeneration", "Normal aging findings; reassure the family that social withdrawal is a natural and healthy part of late life", "Impairments in Transportation and Telephone use; refer the patient for low-vision rehabilitation, set up a simplified high-contrast large-button telephone, and coordinate community-based senior transit services"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Transportation and Telephone use are key IADLs on the Lawton Scale. Visual impairment (from age-related macular degeneration) directly compromises these IADLs, leading to social isolation, depression, and missed medical care. The best intervention is a multidimensional approach: referring to low-vision rehabilitation, adapting the telephone (e.g., large-button, high-contrast, voice-activated), and arranging senior transit or paratransit services to maintain community connection. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 45)'),
